       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. Acetaminophen 2000 mg by mouth three times daily is 6,000 mg/day, and that is a dangerous dose.\n\n- **Daily total:** The usual adult maximum is 4,000 mg/day. Many institutions cap it at 3,000 mg/day. 6,000 mg/day risks serious hepatotoxicity, even with normal liver function.\n- **Single dose:** 2,000 mg is twice the usual maximum single dose of 1,000 mg. For a 70 kg patient, the weight-based limit is about 15 mg/kg, or roughly 1,000 mg.\n\nWas this a typo? Here are some common intended regimens:\n- 1000 mg PO three times daily (3,000 mg/day)\n- 1000 mg PO four times daily (4,000 mg/day, the maximum)\n- 650 mg PO every 6 hours\n\nIf you really do want 2000 mg three times daily, please confirm that and give the clinical rationale. I'll then enter it with a note, though I'd still advise against it.\n\nTell me which regimen you want and I'll enter it."
      }
     ],
